Replay identity and bounded capture
-----------------------------------
Both runs loaded Linux, reached `retired=2,999,999,998`, and stopped with
`exit_kind=budget_exhausted`. For each nonce, full stop objects, UART bytes,
all entry samples and all retained source stride-512 poll samples match the
corresponding automatic source run exactly. UART length is 23,356 bytes in
both runs. The automatic source comparison remains `matches=false` for machine
observations and ordered UART; these exact replays do not change that result.

Each schema-v11 sidecar has two entry-clock records, zero missing ledger
records, zero entry omissions and zero poll omissions. Run 0001 has 43,871
entry hits and 66,395 stride samples; run 0002 has 43,868 entry hits and
66,389 stride samples. Sidecar sizes are 31,788,223 and 31,785,631 bytes.

Callback and completed-quantum values
-------------------------------------
`tsc_hz=1,000,000,000`, and all records have `initial_now_ns=0`. The clock
ledger fields are sampled inside the RDTSC callback before the current quantum
is charged. `quantum_instructions_executed` and
`retired_instructions_through_rdtsc` are attached after that exact set-loop
report returns. For these selected RDTSC instructions the report stop flag
ends the quantum at the RDTSC instruction. The validator checks with checked
arithmetic that:

```text
retired_instructions_through_rdtsc
  == retired_instructions_charged + quantum_instructions_executed
```

| Run | Hit | Callback TSC | Charged now ns | Retired instructions charged | Current quantum instructions | Retired through RDTSC | PM ns | PIT ns | RDTSC ns charged | Idle ns | Quantum | IRQs |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0001 | 1 | `0x6181dbee` | 1,635,900,398 | 1,524,205,398 | 19,366 | 1,524,224,764 | 0 | 16,673,000 | 95,022,000 | 0 | 136,012 | 0 |
| 0002 | 1 | `0x6182ac5c` | 1,635,953,756 | 1,524,256,756 | 19,312 | 1,524,276,068 | 0 | 16,673,000 | 95,024,000 | 0 | 136,014 | 0 |
| 0001 | 2 | `0x61822f6c` | 1,635,921,772 | 1,524,224,772 | 54 | 1,524,224,826 | 0 | 16,673,000 | 95,024,000 | 0 | 136,014 | 0 |
| 0002 | 2 | `0x6182ffa4` | 1,635,975,076 | 1,524,276,076 | 54 | 1,524,276,130 | 0 | 16,673,000 | 95,026,000 | 0 | 136,016 | 0 |

At entry hit 1 the complete retired count through RDTSC differs by
`+51,358 - 54 = +51,304` instructions: the pre-charge retired count differs
by +51,358 and the current quantum in run 0002 contains 54 fewer instructions.
Separately, the observed TSC gap is `+51,358 + 2,000 = +53,358 ns`, from the
pre-charge retired-time delta and already charged RDTSC-time delta. PM, PIT,
idle and delivered IRQ values match. The two pre-charge
clock sums exactly equal their `now_ns` values and have zero unaccounted
elapsed time. Thus the complete retired count is already different by the
first observed entry RDTSC, rather than the +51,358 pre-charge delta being
only a quantum-grouping artifact. This still does not identify which guest
instructions differed or why; it also does not establish nonce causality or
benignity.

At hit 2 the cumulative retired delta remains +51,304; both current quanta
contain 54 instructions. The TSC gap is 53,304 cycles, equal to the +51,304
retired count and +2,000 charged RDTSC ns. This preserves the earlier
entry-to-entry observation without explaining the underlying path.

Final virtual time still differs: 37,404,144,074 versus 37,401,694,078 ns.
The first-two-hit ledger therefore does not explain later machine/UART
variation or the strict comparator failure.

